<header class="gssl-hero">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">GSSL 0.1 · Exploratory runtime</p>
    <h1>A GPU is a place to compute, not a data model.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Keep typed words on a WebGPU device while multiple compute passes work over them. Change the inputs, reuse a pipeline, inspect actual device memory when you choose.</p>
    <p class="scope">This is a <strong>fixed trusted WGSL kernel</strong> run through the generic GSSL resident-session API. The CPU reference is independent JavaScript arithmetic. Neither constitutes a general Lean→WGSL compiler or a formal proof of GPU hardware execution.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="hero-code" aria-label="Compute flow">
    <div class="node">Input <small>host bytes · u32</small></div>
    <div class="arrow">↓ one upload</div>
    <div class="node gpu">GPU pass 1 <small>multiply + add</small></div>
    <div class="arrow">↓ resident buffer</div>
    <div class="node gpu">GPU pass 2 <small>same kernel, other parameters</small></div>
    <div class="arrow">↓ explicit staging readback</div>
    <div class="node">Inspect <small>exact words and CPU comparison</small></div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="gssl-layout" id="workbench">
  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="input-title">
    <div class="panel-head"><h2 id="input-title">Input and program parameters</h2><span>01 · PREPARE</span></div>
    <p>Enter 1–64 unsigned 32-bit integers. The first and second passes apply separate affine transformations modulo 2<sup>32</sup>; the shader stays identical.</p>
    <label for="source">Input words (JSON array)</label>
    <textarea id="source" spellcheck="false" rows="5">[2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19]</textarea>
    <div class="controls-grid">
      <fieldset><legend>First pass</legend>
        <label>Multiply <input id="mul1" type="number" value="3" min="0" max="4294967295" step="1"></label>
        <label>Add <input id="add1" type="number" value="1" min="0" max="4294967295" step="1"></label>
      </fieldset>
      <fieldset><legend>Second pass</legend>
        <label>Multiply <input id="mul2" type="number" value="3" min="0" max="4294967295" step="1"></label>
        <label>Add <input id="add2" type="number" value="1" min="0" max="4294967295" step="1"></label>
      </fieldset>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <button id="run-cpu" type="button" class="secondary">Run CPU reference</button>
      <button id="run-gpu" type="button" class="primary">Run both GPU passes</button>
      <button id="reset-device" type="button" class="secondary" disabled>Release device</button>
    </div>
    <p class="status" id="status" aria-live="polite">CPU reference available. WebGPU capability will be checked when requested.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="output-title">
    <div class="panel-head"><h2 id="output-title">Compute results</h2><span>02 · COMPARE</span></div>
    <p>Expected arithmetic is calculated separately on the CPU. A GPU result appears only after actual device readback. Re-running unchanged inputs should add <em>zero</em> host uploads.</p>
    <div class="result-grid">
      <div><h3>CPU reference</h3><pre id="cpu-result">Not executed</pre></div>
      <div><h3>GPU device readback</h3><pre id="gpu-result">Not executed</pre></div>
    </div>
    <p id="comparison" class="comparison" role="status">No comparison yet.</p>
    <div class="bars" id="word-bars" aria-label="Data bars representing GPU output words"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="resident-title">
    <div class="panel-head"><h2 id="resident-title">What remains on the GPU?</h2><span>03 · RESIDENCY</span></div>
    <p>Input, two parameter blocks, scratch and output are retained across dispatches. The output of pass 1 becomes the input of pass 2; the browser never reads that intermediate between the passes.</p>
    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div><span>Host uploads</span><strong id="stat-uploads">0</strong></div>
      <div><span>Reused uploads</span><strong id="stat-reuses">0</strong></div>
      <div><span>Compiled pipelines</span><strong id="stat-pipelines">0</strong></div>
      <div><span>Compute submissions</span><strong id="stat-submissions">0</strong></div>
      <div><span>Diagnostic readbacks</span><strong id="stat-readbacks">0</strong></div>
      <div><span>Tracked buffer bytes</span><strong id="stat-bytes">0</strong></div>
    </div>
    <details open>
      <summary>Examine a specific resident buffer</summary>
      <div class="inspect-controls">
        <label for="inspect-buffer">Buffer <select id="inspect-buffer">
          <option value="source">Input</option>
          <option value="middle" selected>Intermediate</option>
          <option value="result">Output</option>
          <option value="paramsA">First-pass parameters</option>
          <option value="paramsB">Second-pass parameters</option>
        </select></label>
        <label for="inspect-offset">Offset (words) <input id="inspect-offset" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="0"></label>
        <label for="inspect-count">Count <input id="inspect-count" type="number" min="0" max="64" step="1" value="8"></label>
        <button id="inspect" type="button" class="secondary" disabled>Read selected words</button>
      </div>
      <pre id="inspect-result">No resident GPU buffers.</pre>
      <p>Inspection copies <em>only</em> the requested bounded range to a CPU-readable staging buffer. This is not access to arbitrary browser or operating-system GPU memory.</p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="evidence-title" id="spec">
    <div class="panel-head"><h2 id="evidence-title">GSSL 0.1 · Spec and assurance</h2><span>04 · BOUNDARY</span></div>
    <p><strong>Current core:</strong> typed <code>identity</code>, <code>prim</code>, <code>seq</code>, <code>prod</code>, <code>fanout</code> and <code>map</code>; pure interpretations and theorems for map fusion and primitive agreement.</p>
    <p><strong>Current device profile:</strong> bounded named GPU buffers, exact-byte upload reuse, trusted WGSL compilation, dispatch, fences and opt-in diagnostics.</p>
    <p><strong>Not yet provided:</strong> a general GSSL text parser; generic verified kernel IR and WGSL lowering; proof of shader compiler, driver or hardware; an authenticated SPARQL GPU worklet.</p>
    <div class="spec-links">
      <a href="https://github.com/danbri/factoidal-core/blob/main/docs/specs/gssl-0.1.md">Read full GSSL 0.1 draft specification</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/danbri/factoidal-core/blob/main/formal/lean4/L4Factoidal/GpuDomainSpecificLanguage.lean">Lean core theorem</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/danbri/factoidal-core/blob/main/npm/factoidal/lib/gpu-resident.mjs">Generic GPU runtime</a>
      <a href="{{ '/web/hub/paralliverse/' | url }}">Paralleliverse (separate SPARQL and packing experiments)</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/danbri/factoidal-core/issues/691">Assured SPARQL release gate</a>
    </div>
    <details>
      <summary>Inspect the fixed WGSL kernel used here</summary>
      <pre id="kernel-source">Source loaded with the local module.</pre>
      <p>User parameters change the contents of the parameter buffers; this page does not compile arbitrary pasted WGSL.</p>
    </details>
  </section>
</div>
<noscript>The CPU/GPU comparison requires JavaScript. GPU execution also requires a WebGPU-capable browser on a secure origin.</noscript>
